UPDATE companies SET description='OXOS Medical, Inc. was founded in June 2016 in Atlanta, Georgia, as Micro C, LLC, before adopting its current name. The company was started by Evan M. Ruff, a computer engineer and MBA from Georgia Tech, and Dr. Gregory P. Kolovich, a reconstructive hand surgeon trained at Harvard Medical School. Kolovich''s frustration with the traditional imaging process during surgery sparked the idea for a portable dynamic digital radiography device.

The company develops and manufactures portable X‑ray systems, including the Micro C and the newer MC2, both cleared by the U.S. FDA under 510(k). The devices are designed for low radiation exposure, modular portability, and a no‑fire positioning system that optimizes alignment. A proprietary software module called AiLARA automates dose optimization per the ALARA principle. The team comprises engineers, designers, and clinical advisors, though headcount and facility size are not disclosed.

Testimonials cite use by hand and upper‑extremity surgeons, foot‑and‑ankle specialists, and team physicians for professional sports organizations such as the Jacksonville Jaguars. The systems are marketed for point‑of‑need imaging in diverse care settings.

The founders state the mission is to let anyone, anywhere access radiographic diagnostics at the point of need. They emphasize three pillars: safety through low‑dose profiles, simplicity via portable and modular design, and intelligence through real‑time alignment and automated dose control.', certifications=ARRAY['FDA 510(k) clearance (K252068 for MC2)','FDA 510(k) clearance for Micro C (2021)','FDA clearance for AiLARA system (2021)']::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Portable X-ray device development", "Dynamic Digital Radiography", "FDA 510(k) regulatory clearance", "Automated ALARA dose optimization (AiLARA)", "Point-of-care radiographic imaging", "No-fire positioning system", "Modular device design"], "industries": ["Medical imaging", "Orthopedics", "Pediatrics", "Sports medicine"], "materials": [], "equipment": ["Micro C portable X-ray device", "MC2 portable X-ray device", "AiLARA dose optimization system"], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["FDA 510(k) cleared portable X\u2011ray devices (Micro C and MC2)", "Low radiation dose with maintained image quality", "Portable, modular design for point\u2011of\u2011care use", "No\u2011fire positioning system for optimized alignment", "AiLARA automated ALARA dose optimization"], "facts_used": ["Company name and headquarters: Atlanta, Georgia -> db", "Founded June 2016 as Micro C, LLC -> /about", "Founders: Evan M. Ruff (CEO) and Gregory P. Kolovich, MD (CMO) -> /about", "Origin: frustration with traditional imaging during hand surgery -> /about", "Products: Micro C and MC2 portable X-ray devices -> /about, /", "FDA 510(k) clearances: Micro C (2021), AiLARA (2021), MC2 (2024) -> /about", "Mission: enable radiographic diagnostics at point of need -> /about", "Industries served: orthopedics, pediatrics, sports medicine -> /", "Clinical users: hand surgeons, foot/ankle surgeons, team physicians -> /", "Differentiators: low radiation, portable/modular, no-fire positioning, AiLARA -> /", "Equi